WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.580
Secure code review is the structured examination of source code for security-relevant design, implementation, and configuration issues.

2
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:17.480
It asks whether the code protects data, identity, permissions, secrets, and security-sensitive actions in the way the system intends.

3
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:28.700
A useful review looks past whether the code compiles and asks how the code behaves when inputs are unexpected, users have limited permission, integrations fail, or assumptions are wrong.

4
00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:37.620
Secure code review is related to general code review, static analysis, penetration testing, and threat modeling, but it is not identical to any of them.

5
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:44.040
General code review often focuses on correctness, maintainability, readability, and style.

6
00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.740
Static analysis can identify patterns automatically.

7
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.360
Penetration testing evaluates behavior in a running system.

8
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.660
Threat modeling explores what can go wrong in the design.

9
00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:01.800
Secure code review sits close to implementation and checks whether design intent is actually enforced in code.

10
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.000
The reviewer's attention should follow the security story of the application.

11
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.460
How does data enter?

12
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.480
Where does identity get established?

13
00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.580
Where are permissions checked?

14
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.200
Where do secrets come from?

15
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.860
Which configuration values change behavior?

16
00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.440
Where are trust boundaries crossed?

17
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:27.820
Which code paths touch databases, files, templates, commands, logs, outbound requests, or administrative actions?

18
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.240
These questions keep the review grounded in risk rather than in a generic checklist.

19
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.960
The practice is defensive engineering, not a hunt for blame.

20
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:49.500
Reviewers should look for exploitable conditions, unsafe assumptions, missing controls, and mismatches between design and implementation, then explain them with evidence and practical remediation guidance.

21
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:58.960
The best secure code reviews help teams learn patterns, improve shared libraries, strengthen standards, and prevent the same mistake from appearing in future work.
